# Git attributes — line endings, diff behaviour, and what counts as binary.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL
#
# Without it, Git's line-ending behaviour is decided by each developer's local
# `core.autocrlf`, which is `true` by default on Windows. That default rewrites every
# checked-out text file to CRLF, and two gates in this repository break the moment it does:
#
#   1. `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` (Backend CI, gate 2) enforces `.editorconfig`,
#      which sets `end_of_line = lf`. A CRLF working tree fails that gate on files nobody
#      touched, and the failure reads as "your formatting is wrong" rather than "your clone
#      is wrong", which is the worst kind of CI failure.
#
#   2. `.claude/hooks/*.sh` are committed executable scripts with a `#!/usr/bin/env bash`
#      shebang. A CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter literally named
#      "bash\r", and the script dies with `bad interpreter: No such file or directory`. On
#      WSL, Git Bash, and any Linux container that mounts a Windows clone, that takes the
#      entire agentic harness — every guardrail in it — offline silently.
#
# The rules below are normalisation rules: they tell Git what to store in the repository
# (always LF for text) and what to check out. They are authoritative regardless of anyone's
# `core.autocrlf` setting, which is exactly the point.
#
# AFTER CHANGING THIS FILE, renormalise an existing clone once:
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git status          # review, then commit the line-ending-only changes on their own
#
# The rules here deliberately mirror `.editorconfig`. If you change one, change the other:
# `.editorconfig` governs what editors write, `.gitattributes` governs what Git stores.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default: detect text, and normalise every text file to LF in the repository
# and in the working tree.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git decide text vs binary by content, so an unlisted binary
# format is still safe. `eol=lf` then forces LF on checkout too — not just in
# the object store — which is what keeps a Windows working tree passing
# `dotnet format` and keeps shebangs executable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shell scripts — LF, unconditionally. See reason 2 above.
#
# The explicit paths after the glob are redundant today and deliberate anyway:
# they are the files where a CRLF regression is not a cosmetic annoyance but a
# silent loss of every hook, so they are named rather than left to a pattern.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh              text eol=lf
*.bash            text eol=lf
*.zsh             text eol=lf
.claude/hooks/*   text eol=lf
src/frontend/docker/*.sh text eol=lf

# Files with no extension that are still shell scripts.
*.envrc           text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows-only scripts — CRLF, deliberately.
#
# `cmd.exe` and PowerShell tolerate LF for the most part, but a `.bat` with LF
# endings has genuine, documented failure modes (multi-line `for` blocks and
# trailing `goto :eof`), and `.editorconfig` already declares CRLF for these
# three. This is the one place LF is wrong.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.bat             text eol=crlf
*.cmd             text eol=crlf
*.ps1             text eol=crlf
*.psm1            text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .NET — text, with language-aware diff hunk headers.
#
# `diff=csharp` makes `git diff` label each hunk with the enclosing member
# rather than with whatever brace happened to be nearby.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.cs              text eol=lf diff=csharp
*.csx             text eol=lf diff=csharp
*.csproj          text eol=lf
*.props           text eol=lf
*.targets         text eol=lf
*.slnx            text eol=lf
*.sln             text eol=lf
*.runsettings     text eol=lf
*.ruleset         text eol=lf
*.resx            text eol=lf

# EF Core migrations are generated, but they MUST be reviewed and they are
# source-controlled deliberately (see `.gitignore`'s explicit negation). The
# migration bodies are NOT marked generated below: a collapsed diff on a
# migration is precisely the diff you needed to read.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TypeScript / JavaScript / web assets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.ts              text eol=lf
*.mts             text eol=lf
*.cts             text eol=lf
*.tsx             text eol=lf
*.js              text eol=lf
*.mjs             text eol=lf
*.cjs             text eol=lf
*.jsx             text eol=lf
*.html            text eol=lf diff=html
*.htm             text eol=lf diff=html
*.css             text eol=lf diff=css
*.scss            text eol=lf diff=css
*.less            text eol=lf diff=css
*.vue             text eol=lf
*.svg             text eol=lf
*.map             text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data, configuration, and documentation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.json            text eol=lf
*.jsonc           text eol=lf
*.json5           text eol=lf
*.yml             text eol=lf
*.yaml            text eol=lf
*.toml            text eol=lf
*.xml             text eol=lf
*.csv             text eol=lf
*.sql             text eol=lf
*.md              text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.markdown        text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.txt             text eol=lf
*.http            text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Infrastructure as code and containers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.tf              text eol=lf
*.tfvars          text eol=lf
*.hcl             text eol=lf
*.bicep           text eol=lf
*.bicepparam      text eol=lf
Dockerfile        text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile      text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf
*.conf            text eol=lf
nginx.conf        text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dotfiles that carry no extension. `text=auto` already covers them, but they
# are the files most likely to be hand-edited on Windows, so they are explicit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.gitignore        text eol=lf
.gitattributes    text eol=lf
.editorconfig     text eol=lf
.dockerignore     text eol=lf
.prettierrc       text eol=lf
.prettierignore   text eol=lf
.npmrc            text eol=lf
.nvmrc            text eol=lf
.env.example      text eol=lf
CODEOWNERS        text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary — never normalise, never diff as text.
#
# `binary` is a built-in macro for `-text -diff -merge`. Anything here that
# Git's content detection would have caught anyway is listed because being
# explicit costs nothing and a corrupted binary from a bad normalisation is
# expensive.
#
# `git check-attr -a` will still report `eol: lf` on these paths, inherited from
# the `*` line at the top. That is reporting, not behaviour: end-of-line
# conversion is skipped entirely once `text` is unset, so `eol` is never
# consulted for a binary. Do not "fix" it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png             binary
*.jpg             binary
*.jpeg            binary
*.gif             binary
*.ico             binary
*.webp            binary
*.avif            binary
*.bmp             binary
*.tif             binary
*.tiff            binary
*.psd             binary

*.woff            binary
*.woff2           binary
*.ttf             binary
*.otf             binary
*.eot             binary

*.pdf             binary
*.zip             binary
*.gz              binary
*.tgz             binary
*.bz2             binary
*.xz              binary
*.7z              binary
*.rar             binary
*.tar             binary

*.dll             binary
*.exe             binary
*.pdb             binary
*.so              binary
*.dylib           binary
*.nupkg           binary
*.snupkg          binary
*.snk             binary
*.mp4             binary
*.webm            binary
*.mp3             binary
*.wav             binary

# Key and certificate material must never be normalised. It should never be
# committed either — `.gitignore` blocks it and `.claude/hooks/secret-scan.sh`
# blocks it again — but a normalised private key is a uniquely bad failure, so
# the third layer is cheap.
*.pfx             binary
*.p12             binary
*.pem             binary
*.der             binary
*.cer             binary
*.crt             binary
*.key             binary

# SQLite / LocalDB artefacts, and the Nx daemon database.
*.db              binary
*.db-shm          binary
*.db-wal          binary
*.mdf             binary
*.ldf             binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated files.
#
# `linguist-generated=true` collapses these in GitHub diffs and excludes them
# from the repository's language statistics. It does NOT stop them being
# reviewed when they matter — it stops a 20,000-line lockfile burying the four
# lines of real change in the same pull request.
#
# NOTE ON MERGING LOCKFILES: never resolve a `package-lock.json` conflict by
# hand and never set a merge driver for it. Take either side, then re-run
# `npm install` and commit the regenerated file. A hand-merged lockfile
# describes a dependency graph that npm never resolved and nobody has run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
package-lock.json       linguist-generated=true
npm-shrinkwrap.json     linguist-generated=true
yarn.lock               linguist-generated=true
pnpm-lock.yaml          linguist-generated=true
*.Designer.cs           linguist-generated=true
**/Migrations/*ModelSnapshot.cs linguist-generated=true

# Generated OpenAPI types. Source-controlled on purpose (the frontend must build
# without a running API) but regenerated, never hand-edited — see ADR-0004.
src/frontend/libs/data-access/api-types/src/lib/types.ts linguist-generated=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Repository language statistics. Cosmetic, but a boilerplate whose GitHub
# language bar reads "HTML 61%" because of a checked-in report tells the reader
# something false about what it is.
#
# No `linguist-language` override on `infra/`: Linguist already identifies `.tf`
# as HCL and `.bicep` as Bicep, and forcing one language across a directory that
# holds both would be a lie about half of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/**           linguist-documentation
*.md              linguist-documentation
